WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.772 --> 00:00:07.976
Grandpa heard Laurie in the hallway humming a tune from Tchaikovsky's Nutcracker Suite.

2
00:00:08.656 --> 00:00:12.118
Laurie skipped into the study.

3
00:00:12.258 --> 00:00:15.600
"Oh, Grandpa, you should have come with Mom and Dad and me!

4
00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:31.878
The Nutcracker ballet was so good!"

5
00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:34.060
"I wish I could have.

6
00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:37.063
But I had a lot of work to do, Laurie."

7
00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:38.804
Grandpa smiled warmly.

8
00:00:38.824 --> 00:00:41.046
"What's that you've got?"

9
00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:44.769
He pointed to the colorful book Laurie was holding.

10
00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:47.571
"They were selling this at the theater.

11
00:00:48.512 --> 00:00:52.675
It retells the story of the Nutcracker and has photos from the ballet.

12
00:00:53.576 --> 00:00:57.820
Look!" She showed Grandpa a picture of a ballet dancer in a pink tutu.

13
00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:00.825
"I saw her dance today!"

14
00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:05.987
She pointed to a male dancer in a large, furry black hat.

15
00:01:06.007 --> 00:01:08.728
"He was my favorite!"

16
00:01:08.748 --> 00:01:11.869
"Ha! The Cossack dancer!"

17
00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:15.751
"I think they called him the Ukrainian dancer.

18
00:01:16.811 --> 00:01:18.692
He did amazing jumps!"

19
00:01:19.892 --> 00:01:27.355
"Yes, the Cossacks, or the horsemen of the wild steppes, as they are sometimes called, are well-known for

20
00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:28.676
their energetic dancing!

21
00:01:29.804 --> 00:01:36.149
They were a rough group of nomadic fighters who lived in the unsettled land between the Volga, Don,

22
00:01:36.269 --> 00:01:39.431
and Dnieper Rivers of modern-day Russia and Ukraine."

23
00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:44.875
"Fighters! Actually the dancer did have a sword."

24
00:01:45.235 --> 00:01:46.136
Laurie remembered.

25
00:01:46.156 --> 00:01:48.137
"It was fake though.

26
00:01:49.218 --> 00:01:50.539
What were they fighting for?"

27
00:01:50.559 --> 00:01:57.604
"Well, many of them fought for Russia, who was trying to gain control over that area.

28
00:01:58.404 --> 00:02:00.185
Russia is huge now, isn't it?

29
00:02:01.266 --> 00:02:06.588
But like many other countries, it started out small and fought a lot to gain its territory.

30
00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:15.133
Way back in the 800s, royal Viking families began to form states and cities around those three rivers.

31
00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:22.056
One of the first states to be established was called Kievan Rus.

32
00:02:22.076 --> 00:02:29.499
From the ninth century to the twelfth century, Kievan Rus grew to become the largest, wealthiest state in

33
00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:30.079
the area."

34
00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:34.440
"Does the name 'Russia' come from 'Kievan Rus'?"

35
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.521
Laurie asked.

36
00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:37.301
"It does!

37
00:02:38.502 --> 00:02:39.862
But it wasn't Russia yet.

38
00:02:41.102 --> 00:02:45.304
In the 1200s Genghis Khan and the Mongols invaded.

39
00:02:46.456 --> 00:02:51.818
Kievan Rus remained part of the Mongol Empire until the late 1400s.

40
00:02:53.099 --> 00:03:00.622
Then a prince from the rich trading city of Moscow organized the local people to resist Mongol rule.

41
00:03:01.682 --> 00:03:04.343
This prince was named Ivan III.

42
00:03:05.744 --> 00:03:11.987
Later, because he had freed his people from the Mongols, he became known as Ivan the Great."

43
00:03:12.007 --> 00:03:14.948
"So now was it Russia?"

44
00:03:15.292 --> 00:03:16.393
Laurie wanted to know.

45
00:03:16.413 --> 00:03:21.215
"Sure, now we can finally start calling it Russia.

46
00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:31.620
We could even call it the Russian Empire because Ivan IV—Ivan the Great's grandson—named himself Russia's first czar

47
00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.061
in 1547.

48
00:03:34.721 --> 00:03:39.744
Ivan the IV was a strong ruler who conquered lots of territory for Russia.

49
00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:46.538
But he is remembered as Ivan the Terrible because his rule was also one of the bloodiest in

50
00:03:46.618 --> 00:03:47.458
Russian history.

51
00:03:48.538 --> 00:03:52.880
You see, Ivan was incredibly worried about losing his authority.

52
00:03:54.120 --> 00:04:00.762
This made him wary of everyone, especially the rich Russian landowners, who held a lot of power.

53
00:04:02.103 --> 00:04:05.844
So he sent loyal servants to seize the noblemen's lands.

54
00:04:07.124 --> 00:04:12.046
And to guarantee that they would never fight him for it, he executed the landowners!

55
00:04:13.627 --> 00:04:16.868
Ivan the Terrible wanted to expand Russia's borders.

56
00:04:18.028 --> 00:04:26.132
He was particularly interested in defeating the Mongol hordes to the east and expanding Russia's territory all the

57
00:04:26.172 --> 00:04:29.153
way down the Volga River to the Caspian Sea.

58
00:04:30.173 --> 00:04:33.575
To do this he needed lots of men willing to fight for him."

59
00:04:34.835 --> 00:04:35.335
"I know!"

60
00:04:35.755 --> 00:04:36.736
Laurie blurted out.

61
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.162
"He used the Cossacks!"

62
00:04:39.642 --> 00:04:42.865
Laurie jumped up and pulled an imaginary sword from her hip.

63
00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:46.048
"Right!" said Grandpa.

64
00:04:47.249 --> 00:04:51.653
"Luckily for Ivan, those wild horsemen lived right on the Russian borders.

65
00:04:52.714 --> 00:04:56.058
'Cossack' comes from a word meaning free man.

66
00:04:57.319 --> 00:05:02.544
These men were considered free because they didn't belong to any of the states in the area.

67
00:05:03.648 --> 00:05:08.692
This meant they didn't follow any ruler's laws and didn't pay taxes to anyone.

68
00:05:09.973 --> 00:05:16.297
That probably doesn't sound like much to you, Laurie," Grandpa added, "but to adults that is a very

69
00:05:16.377 --> 00:05:17.078
big deal.

70
00:05:18.438 --> 00:05:24.062
Anyway, these Cossacks were usually peasant men who had left their original villages.

71
00:05:25.223 --> 00:05:30.927
They didn't like what life there had to offer them—most peasants in the Middle Ages lived practically as

72
00:05:31.027 --> 00:05:40.130
slaves. Out in the wild steppes, they became strong and excellent fighters—exactly what Ivan the Terrible needed.

73
00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.431
He hired these Cossacks to fight the Mongols.

74
00:05:45.492 --> 00:05:50.313
In exchange he promised to pay them and grant them continued independence.

75
00:05:51.453 --> 00:05:55.034
From then on the Cossacks became known as mercenaries.

76
00:05:56.175 --> 00:05:57.895
Do you know what a mercenary is?"

77
00:05:59.216 --> 00:06:00.316
Laurie shook her head.

78
00:06:01.356 --> 00:06:04.677
"A mercenary is someone who fights for pay.

79
00:06:05.858 --> 00:06:11.640
Besides the money they receive, they have no interest in the battles they fight," Grandpa explained.

80
00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:20.504
"Even after Ivan the Terrible, Russian rulers continued to employ the Cossacks, and they were responsible for helping

81
00:06:20.584 --> 00:06:25.626
Russia expand and protect its borders well into the eighteenth century."

82
00:06:26.887 --> 00:06:32.673
"Well, they couldn't have been fighting all the time," Laurie remarked, glancing at her Nutcracker book.

83
00:06:33.814 --> 00:06:34.934
"When did they dance?"

84
00:06:34.954 --> 00:06:36.975
"Good point.

85
00:06:38.095 --> 00:06:45.418
When they weren't fighting, the Cossacks liked to hunt, fish, drink, play music, and dance.

86
00:06:46.399 --> 00:06:50.980
Their dances became famous because of their incredible acrobatics.

87
00:06:52.581 --> 00:06:58.763
Cossack men were strong and fit, and they showed off their abilities by leaping into the air again

88
00:06:58.864 --> 00:06:59.464
and again."

89
00:07:01.184 --> 00:07:01.805
"Like this?"

90
00:07:03.146 --> 00:07:06.489
Laurie squatted down and then jumped up, kicking one leg out.

91
00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:10.252
"Yes! And like this!"

92
00:07:11.493 --> 00:07:16.958
Grandpa too jumped from his chair, crossed his arms in front of him, and kicked his feet out

93
00:07:17.118 --> 00:07:17.779
one at a time.

94
00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:25.085
Then the two linked arms, and began singing the theme from the Nutcracker Suite as they danced around

95
00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:25.686
the study.

96
00:07:28.332 --> 00:07:33.638
"One day we'll have to go to Ukraine and see a real performance by modern Cossacks!"

97
00:07:34.098 --> 00:07:35.700
Grandpa shouted joyfully.

98
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.503
"Yeah!" Laurie answered.

99
00:07:40.285 --> 00:07:41.746
Suddenly there was a loud knock.

100
00:07:43.007 --> 00:07:45.170
"What on earth is all that racket?"

101
00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.352
Mom demanded from outside the door.

102
00:07:48.553 --> 00:07:51.236
Grandpa and Laurie abruptly stopped dancing.

103
00:07:52.192 --> 00:07:54.795
Then they looked at each other and burst out laughing.

104
00:07:54.815 --> 00:08:01.561
"For a 65-year-old archaeologist, Grandpa is pretty fun to hang out with," Laurie thought.

105
00:08:02.622 --> 00:08:04.884
And she was glad to be his granddaughter.
